    "body": "### Problem\n\nI don't know about terminal internals well enough to say if this is a Neovim bug, tmux bug, or an interaction between the two. I found #17285 and #39803 while browsing the tracker and wondered if they're related.\n\nNeovim doesn't pick up pane resizes under tmux control mode, and the two directions fail differently:\n\n- Widening the pane: Neovim's layout stays exactly the same - it doesn't expand into the new space.\n- Narrowing the pane: Neovim's layout becomes garbled instead of shrinking cleanly.\n\nOther programs in the same pane (`top`, `tig`, plain `vim`) resize correctly in both directions, so this seems specific to Neovim. Reproduced consistently on both iTerm2 and RootShell, using each one's tmux control mode integration.\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n1. Open a terminal with tmux control mode enabled (tested on iTerm2 and RootShell).\n2. Start a tmux session, open two panes side-by-side, run `nvim --clean` in the left pane.\n3. Widen the pane, observe Neovim's layout does not expand, staying at the old width.\n4. Narrow the pane, observe Neovim's layout becomes garbled rather than cleanly shrinking.\n\nAdditional checks:\n\n- `stty size` and `tmux display -p '#{pane_width}x#{pane_height}'` report the new, correct dimensions after either resize.\n- `kill -WINCH <nvim_pid>` (sent from another pane) has no effect.\n- `kill -TSTP <nvim_pid>` followed by `kill -CONT <nvim_pid>` has no effect.\n- Manually suspending/resuming the job myself in the shell (Ctrl-Z, then `fg`) fixes it immediately, and Neovim redraws at the correct size.\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nNeovim should resize to match the new pane dimensions in both directions - expanding when the pane is widened and shrinking cleanly when narrowed - the same way `top`, `tig`, and plain `vim` do in the same pane.\n\n### Nvim version (nvim -v)\n\n0.12.4\n\n### Vim (not Nvim) behaves the same?\n\nno, vim 9.1.1752\n\n### Operating system/version\n\nmacOS 26.5.1\n\n### Terminal name/version\n\nrootshell 1.0.10 (105), iTerm2 3.6.11, tmux 3.6b/3.7b\n\n### $TERM environment variable\n\ntmux-256color\n\n### Installation\n\nhomebrew",
